# Strict TDD Rules

For every behavior change, use Red → Green → Refactor.

## Red
- First inspect relevant existing tests, implementation, and test commands.
- Write or modify tests only.
- Do not modify production code.
- Run the smallest relevant test command.
- Confirm the new test fails for the expected behavioral reason.
- If the test fails because of setup, import, syntax, or environment issues,
  fix the test setup first; do not call it a valid Red state.

## Green
- Only after a valid Red state, write the smallest production-code change
  needed to make the failing test pass.
- Do not weaken assertions, alter expected outputs, or change fixtures merely
  to make tests pass.
- Do not add untested features or perform unrelated refactors.
- Run the relevant tests after implementation.

## Refactor
- Only refactor when all relevant tests pass.
- Preserve externally observable behavior.
- Run relevant tests again after refactoring.

## General
- Work on one small observable behavior per loop.
- State the exact test command run and its result.
- Do not edit unrelated files.
- Prefer behavior-focused tests over tests coupled to private implementation.
- Before significant edits, inspect git diff; after edits, report changed files.
